  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of interfacial chemistry on a metal foil electrode covered by two surface layers. A dark base labeled metal foil supports an intermediate layer labeled Li_xN_yH_z, which is itself covered by a light blue SEI layer. Arrows marked e− rise from the metal through the overlying layers, indicating electron transport from the electrode to the interface.\n\nAbove the surface, lithium ions and a nitrile-containing alcohol appear to approach the electrode, while a curved reaction arrow suggests that the alcohol is converted into an alkoxide species paired with NH3. A five-membered cyclic ether is also shown near the surface, implying solvent participation or SEI-related chemistry. Overall, the image conveys that the buried nitride/hydride-like interphase and the SEI together mediate electron transfer and chemical transformation of electrolyte or additive molecules at the metal surface.\n\nThe main message seems to be that a structured, multilayer interphase on lithium metal enables reduction reactions while protecting the underlying foil. The figure emphasizes the coupling between ion/electron transport, interphase composition, and the formation of surface reaction products rather than providing quantitative data.",
